  <p>
    Richard Feynman, one of the 20th century's most brilliant and charismatic physicists, had a unique perspective
    on gravity that combined mathematical rigor with philosophical humility. While he made fundamental contributions
    to quantum mechanics that would later prove essential for quantum gravity research, Feynman maintained a healthy
    skepticism about theories that outpaced experimental evidence. His views on gravity—particularly his famous
    admission that nobody truly understands "why" gravity exists—continue to resonate with physicists today.
  </p>

  <h2>Who Was Richard Feynman?</h2>
  <p>
    Richard Phillips Feynman (1918-1988) was an American theoretical physicist who won the Nobel Prize in Physics
    in 1965 for his work on quantum electrodynamics (QED). Beyond his technical achievements, Feynman became famous
    for his ability to explain complex physics concepts in simple, accessible language. His lectures, books, and
    personality made him a scientific icon whose influence extends far beyond his formal research contributions.
  </p>

  <h3>Feynman's Major Contributions to Physics</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Quantum Electrodynamics (QED): Developed the theory describing how light and matter interact</li>
    <li>Path Integral Formulation: Created a new way to calculate quantum mechanical probabilities</li>
    <li>Feynman Diagrams: Invented visual tools that revolutionized how physicists calculate particle interactions</li>
    <li>Parton Model: Proposed the existence of quarks inside protons and neutrons</li>
    <li>Quantum Computing Foundations: Early pioneer in quantum computing concepts</li>
    <li>Challenger Investigation: Famously demonstrated the cause of the Space Shuttle Challenger disaster</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Feynman's Stance on Gravity: We Know How, Not Why</h2>
  <p>
    One of Feynman's most famous statements about gravity came during his renowned "Messenger Lectures" at Cornell
    University in 1964. When asked to explain gravity, Feynman gave an answer that surprised many listeners and
    revealed his philosophical approach to physics.
  </p>

  <h3>The Famous "Why" Question</h3>
  <p>
    Feynman explained that physicists can describe with remarkable precision how gravity works—the mathematical laws
    governing gravitational attraction—but cannot answer the deeper question of why gravity exists or why it takes
    the form it does. He stated: "I can't explain that attraction in terms of anything else that's familiar to you.
    For example, if we said the magnets attract like rubber bands, I would be cheating you. Because they're not like
    rubber bands."
  </p>

  <p>
    This philosophical position reflected Feynman's understanding that all physical explanations eventually bottom
    out at fundamental laws that we can measure and mathematically describe but cannot reduce to simpler concepts.
    Gravity, he argued, is one of these fundamental aspects of reality.
  </p>

