  <p>
    <strong>Key Takeaway:</strong> Richard Feynman's perspective on gravity combined mathematical sophistication
    with philosophical humility. While he developed essential tools for quantum gravity research—particularly path
    integrals—he remained skeptical of theories that outpaced experimental evidence. His famous acknowledgment that
    we can describe how gravity works but not truly explain why it exists remains a profound insight. Feynman's
    approach—rigorous yet playful, skeptical yet open-minded—continues to inspire physicists tackling gravity's
    deepest mysteries. His legacy reminds us that physics is ultimately an empirical science, and even our most
    beautiful theories must face nature's judgment through observation and experiment.
  </p>
